ANSWER: The sky appears blue because air molecules scatter sunlight in all directions, a process called Rayleigh scattering, which is much more effective for shorter (blue) wavelengths than longer (red) ones. As a result, we see the scattered blue light from all parts of the sky, while the unscattered red light passes straight through.
